nt","key":"lLuVB4liGf"}],"kind":"definitionTerm","template":"{name}","resolved":true,"html_id":"term-disjoint-events","remote":true,"url":"/pages/glossary","dataUrl":"/pages.glossary.json","key":"H5HfyHpM0w"},{"type":"text","value":" or ","position":{"start":{"line":41,"column":1},"end":{"line":41,"column":1}},"key":"IP1tD6TJ8z"},{"type":"crossReference","label":"disjoint events","identifier":"term-disjoint events","children":[{"type":"text","value":"mutually exclusive","key":"ECdogtS1Tu"}],"kind":"definitionTerm","template":"{name}","resolved":true,"html_id":"term-disjoint-events","remote":true,"url":"/pages/glossary","dataUrl":"/pages.glossary.json","key":"fGk1vlcIDd"},{"type":"text","value":". (We will use disjoint.)","position":{"start":{"line":41,"column":1},"end":{"line":41,"column":1}},"key":"ZOWTCnW40b"}],"key":"oluZFcf1nE"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"We can therefore say that in the first example events A and B are disjoint, and in the second example they are not disjoint. Using Venn diagrams, we can visualize two events that are disjoint and compare them to two events that are not:","position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"key":"USwFIIS0Xw"}],"key":"pk8hhdAIZE"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/textbook/build/m09-disjoint-venn-53f4e4adf5804c6e5dd931f149143592.svg","alt":"Two Venn diagrams side by side. In the first, labeled A and B are disjoint, the two circles are completely separate, so the events cannot occur together. In the second, labeled A and B are not disjoint, the two circles partially overlap, so the events can occur at the same time.","key":"yJw56SfzEO","urlSource":"images/gen/m09-disjoint-venn.svg"}],"enumerator":"1","key":"Mn06mJhnth"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":49,"column":1},"end":{"line":49,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Venn diagrams suggest that another way to think about disjoint versus not disjoint events is that disjoint events ","position":{"start":{"line":49,"column":1},"end":{"line":49,"column":1}},"key":"O7qZT1tnH6"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":49,"column":1},"end":{"line":49,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"do not overlap","position":{"start":{"line":49,"column":1},"end":{"line":49,"column":1}},"key":"j5vnMtqp8y"}],"key":"XxcmlTx72P"},{"type":"text","value":". They do not share any of the possible outcomes, and therefore cannot happen together. On the other hand, events that are not disjoint are overlapping in the sense that they share some of the possible outcomes and therefore can occur at the same time.","position":{"start":{"line":49,"column":1},"end":{"line":49,"column":1}},"key":"Om6qOO0P5p"}],"key":"MqPAx7Ael9"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":51,"column":1},"end":{"line":51,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The purpose of the following activity is to strengthen your intuition and understanding about disjoint versus not disjoint events.","position":{"start":{"line":51,"column":1},"end":{"line":51,"column":1}},"key":"n5kcmlP3Jb"}],"key":"uH6JiDmvTn"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":53,"column":1},"end":{"line":53,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Check Your Understanding: Disjoint Events","position":{"start":{"line":53,"column":1},"end":{"line":53,"column":1}},"key":"tAJBeKqSQH"}],"identifier":"check-your-understanding-disjoint-events","label":"Check Your Understanding: Disjoint Events","html_id":"check-your-understanding-disjoint-events","implicit":true,"key":"BFvu3TcHbZ"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":55,"column":1},"end":{"line":55,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Recall the couple that is planning to have 3 children, where the sample space S of all possible outcomes is:","position":{"start":{"line":55,"column":1},"end":{"line":55,"column":1}},"key":"YwZl0CfWQD"}],"key":"Y3sUhSYsTl"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":57,"column":1},"end":{"line":57,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"S = {BBB, BBG, BGB, GBB, GGB, GBG, BGG, GGG}","position":{"start":{"line":57,"column":1},"end":{"line":57,"column":1}},"key":"SK3cin8dKD"}],"key":"I4UFCKhLdR"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-73eca877a522c5138994c2b9b27d024e.mjs","model":{"question":"Consider the events A: \"the couple has at most one girl\" and B: \"the couple has 3 girls.\" Are these events disjoint?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"Yes—they share no outcomes, so they are disjoint","feedback":"Correct! A contains outcomes with zero or one girl, while B is GGG—they share no outcomes, so they cannot occur together."},{"correct":false,"text":"No—they can occur together","feedback":"Check again: no outcome has both at most one girl and three girls."}],"multi":false,"hint":"List the outcomes in each event and check for overlap. A = {BBB, BBG, BGB, GBB}; B = {GGG}.","explanation":""},"key":"rL9d2DoRNO"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-73eca877a522c5138994c2b9b27d024e.mjs","model":{"question":"Now consider events C: \"the first child is a boy\" and D: \"the couple has exactly one girl.\" Are these events disjoint?","choices":[{"correct":false,"text":"Yes—they are disjoint","feedback":"The two events do share outcomes—look at BBG and BGB."},{"correct":true,"text":"No—they share the outcomes BBG and BGB, so they are not disjoint","feedback":"Correct! The outcomes BBG and BGB are in both events, so C and D can occur together—they are not disjoint."}],"multi":false,"hint":"C = {BBB, BBG, BGB, BGG}; D = {BBG, BGB, GBB}. Do they share any outcomes?","explanation":""},"key":"GqRpzNNPk7"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-73eca877a522c5138994c2b9b27d024e.mjs","model":{"question":"Which of the following pairs of events is ALWAYS disjoint, for any event A?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"A and \"not A\"","feedback":"Correct! An event and its complement can never occur together (and together they cover all of S)."},{"correct":false,"text":"Any two different events","feedback":"Two different events can easily overlap—like \"type A blood\" and \"is a woman.\""},{"correct":false,"text":"A and A","feedback":"An event always overlaps with itself."}],"multi":false,"hint":"Think of an event and its complement.","explanation":""},"key":"q2gK9TF6vn"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":85,"column":1},"end":{"line":85,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Now that we understand the idea of disjoint events, we can finally get to rule 4. Rule 4 actually has two versions, one for finding P(A or B) in the special case when events A and B are disjoint, and a more general version for when the events are not necessarily disjoint. We will first present the version of rule 4 that is restricted to disjoint events, and later in the module (after rule 5) we will revisit rule 4 and present the more general version.","position":{"start":{"line":85,"column":1},"end":{"line":85,"column":1}},"key":"zczfk2e2Rm"}],"key":"uygdD94tPT"}],"key":"oEqCKsRrUL"}],"key":"ya2DgEJ8TG"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"The Complement Rule: The Other Side of the Coin","url":"/pages/m09-probability-rules-2-of-11","group":"Section 6: Finding Probability of Events"},"next":{"title":"The Addition Rule for Disjoint Events: Finding P(A or B)","url":"/pages/m09-probability-rules-4-of-11","group":"Section 6: Finding Probability of Events"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3000"}